# gitleaks — fingerprints deliberately ignored, one per line as `commit:file:rule:line`.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL (M48, 2026-08-31). `ci.yml`'s secret scan runs
# `gitleaks detect --source .`, which scans git HISTORY rather than the working tree. The
# `static-analysis` checkout used `fetch-depth: 2` — chosen in M40 for tracker-lint's R7 — so the clone
# held two commits and THE SECRET SCAN ON A PUBLIC REPOSITORY WAS CHECKING TWO COMMITS AT A TIME FOR
# ITS WHOLE LIFE. Raising the depth to 0 for R7 made it scan 818 commits, and it immediately reported
# three findings that had been unreachable. That is the same vacuous-success shape this project
# catalogues elsewhere: a gate reporting `passed` while it could not see.
#
# ⛔ ALL THREE ARE ONE STRING, AND IT IS NOT A SECRET. Verified before this file was written, by
# comparing the sha256 of each finding's value: identical. It is the password fixture in
# PasswordStrength.test.ts — a password-STRENGTH checklist has to be asserted against something that
# looks like a password — quoted twice into the tracker on the day that component shipped.
#
# ⛔ THE TEST LINE ALREADY CARRIES `// gitleaks:allow`, AND THAT IS NOT ENOUGH, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH
# UNDERSTANDING. An inline directive suppresses the match in the commit where the directive EXISTS.
# History still contains the commits that predate it, and those commits can never be edited without
# rewriting the repository. An inline allow is a working-tree instrument; a fingerprint is the
# historical one. Both are needed, and neither substitutes for the other.
#
# ⛔ WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY NOT DONE: `--no-git` (scans the worktree and gives back the history coverage
# this increment just switched on) and disabling the `generic-api-key` rule (blinds the scan to a whole
# class). A fingerprint is `commit:file:rule:line` and matches nothing else, so it is the narrowest
# instrument available — and a NEW leak in these same files still fails, because the line and commit
# would differ.
#
# Adding a line here is a security decision. State which string, and why it is not a secret.

